<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	M9: iOS-only entitlements (App Group + Keychain Access Group, shared
	with the NotificationService Extension — see Config/Shared.xcconfig's
	doc comment on OTEGAMI_APP_GROUP/OTEGAMI_KEYCHAIN_GROUP). Applied only
	to the iOS/iOS Simulator SDKs (project.yml:
	CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS[sdk=iphoneos*]/[sdk=iphonesimulator*]), *not*
	macOS: NotificationService is iOS-only (its own doc comment explains
	why), and requesting an App Group capability macOS never uses would
	require it to be portal-registered before `make mac`'s automatic
	signing can produce a valid provisioning profile — unnecessary friction
	for a capability nothing on that platform reads. `AppDatabase`/
	`KeychainCredentialStore` already degrade gracefully (fall back to the
	pre-M9 per-app storage) when an App Group/Keychain Access Group is
	named but not actually entitled, so macOS simply keeps behaving as it
	did before M9.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(OTEGAMI_APP_GROUP)</string>
	</array>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(OTEGAMI_KEYCHAIN_GROUP)</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	Required for UIApplication.registerForRemoteNotifications() to
	actually obtain a real device token on a physical device (moot on the
	simulator — see PushTokenCenter.swift's doc comment). This static
	"development" value is only ever meaningful for an unsigned/
	locally-signed build — for a real Development/Ad Hoc/Distribution
	provisioning profile, Xcode's automatic signing overwrites this key
	at sign time to whatever the assigned profile actually grants (a
	Distribution profile only ever grants "production"), which is why
	this file staying "development" hasn't blocked archiving/signing
	TestFlight builds. What environment gets *sent* to otegami-relay at
	push-registration time is decided at runtime instead, by reading the
	actual signed-in aps-environment out of the running binary's own
	embedded provisioning profile — see
	APNSEnvironmentDetector.detectedEnvironment(bundle:) (Task #57).
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<!--
	M11 (iCloud account sync, docs/icloud-sync.md): lets this app read/write
	its own private NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore key-value pairs, synced by
	iCloud to every device signed into the same Apple ID — the transport
	AccountCloudSyncEngine uses for the "accounts.v1" payload. Unlike the
	App Group above, this doesn't require a separately-registered
	container id (KVS rides on the app's own iCloud entitlement, not a
	CloudKit container) — see project.yml's doc comment on why this key
	*is* applied to both iOS and macOS (unlike App Group/Keychain Access
	Group above), and Otegami-macOS.entitlements for the macOS copy of
	just this one key.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
	<string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
	<!--
	Communication Notifications (iOS-only, Task: 送信者アバター付きプッシュ
	通知): lets the app donate an `INSendMessageIntent` (with a sender
	`INPerson`/avatar image, see NotificationService/CommunicationNotification.swift)
	so the OS renders an incoming mail's push notification as a large
	circular sender avatar with this app's icon automatically composited
	into its bottom-right corner, instead of the plain app-icon banner.
	Without this key granted, `content.updating(from:)` (called from the
	main app's own donation paths, and — the primary user of this — the
	NotificationService Extension's `deliver()`) silently fails to apply the
	decoration; the notification still delivers, just undecorated. Apple's
	own docs are explicit that this capability must be requested and
	enabled for this App ID in the Developer Portal *before* a build
	carrying this key can even sign — if a signing failure ever appears
	after adding this key, check that first. **Not added to
	Otegami-macOS.entitlements**: Communication Notifications are an iOS/
	iPadOS/watchOS feature (Apple's own `UNNotificationContent.updating(from:)`
	docs), and NotificationService (the only place this repo builds an
	`INSendMessageIntent`) is an iOS-only extension target to begin with
	(this file's own doc comment on why App Group/Keychain Access Group stay
	iOS-only says the same about NotificationService's platform scope).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.communication</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	Task #48 (デフォルトメールアプリ対応): this file's `com.apple.developer
	.mail-client`-carrying counterpart is Otegami-iOS-MailClient.entitlements,
	swapped in via the `OTEGAMI_MAIL_CLIENT_ENTITLEMENT` xcconfig flag
	(Config/Shared.xcconfig) rather than added here directly — see that
	file's doc comment for why. Keep the two files' shared keys above in
	sync by hand if either changes.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
